t. Immediate protective action was appropriate and necessary.\n\nRECOMMENDATION: Maintain protective custody pending thorough investigation. Full skeletal survey essential to identify any additional fractures. Forensic interview to obtain child\'s account in safe environment. Law enforcement investigation concurrent with CPS assessment. Both parents require comprehensive evaluation. Given severity, criminal charges likely appropriate. Reunification should only be considered after extensive therapy, safety planning, and demonstrated sustained change - if at all. Child may need long-term therapeutic support.\n\n================================================================================\nCASE F - Thompson Family (Case #2025-04-1928)\n================================================================================\n\nRISK SCORE: 42/100 (LOW-MODERATE)\n\nCASE SUMMARY:\nNoah Thompson (age 6) reported with small bruise on upper arm. Mother stated she grabbed him hard when he ran into street toward traffic, expressing immediate remorse. Second CPS report - prior substantiated neglect (Jan 2025) for inadequate supervision and food insecurity. Family currently receiving Intensive Family Services with documented exceptional progress across all domains. Mother engaged with therapy, parenting classes completed, food security established, children thriving.\n\nRISK FACTOR ANALYSIS:\n\n1. Evidence of Current Harm: LOW\n   - Minor bruising (1.5 inches, light purple, fresh)\n   - Clear protective context (preventing child from traffic danger)\n   - Mother\'s spontaneous acknowledgment and remorse\n   - Injury consistent with described circumstances\n\n2. Caregiver Capacity: STRONG\n   - Highly engaged with ALL services (100% attendance)\n   - Completed parenting group successfully\n   - Mental health treatment showing significant improvement\n   - Employed part-time, building self-sufficiency\n   - Demonstrated insight and accountability\n\n3. Home Environment Stability: IMPROVED/STABLE\n   - Adequate housing\n   - Food security established (WIC, SNAP, confirmed by services)\n   - Clean, organized home environment\n   - Children consistently supervised\n\n4. Child Vulnerability: LOW\n   - Age 6, thriving in kindergarten (97% attendance)\n   - No behavioral or academic concerns\n   - Regular childcare providing stability\n   - Siblings also doing well\n\n5. Family Support Systems: MODERATE-STRONG\n   - Maternal aunt actively involved and available\n   - Strong service team (Intensive Family Services, therapist, CPS worker)\n   - Children in supportive childcare environment\n   - Mother has developed coping skills and insight\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nWhile this represents a second report within four months, the context differs significantly from the prior substantiated neglect. The current incident occurred in a protective context with mother reacting to immediate safety threat. Extensive collateral documentation confirms mother\'s exceptional progress across all service domains. Multiple providers (Intensive Family Services worker, therapist, school, childcare) confirm positive changes and children\'s thriving status. Mother\'s transparency and emotional response indicate accountability rather than minimization. The service infrastructure currently in place provides robust monitoring and support capacity. Risk appears to be residual from prior circumstances rather than indication of new pattern.\n\nRECOMMENDATION: Acknowledge report but continue current service plan without escalation. Use upcoming service team meeting to assess mother\'s current stress level and support needs. Minor injury in protective context does not warrant service modification given documented trajectory of improvement. Consider this incident in context of 4-month positive progression when making disposition decisions. Continue planned step-down discussion as appropriate.\n\n================================================================================\nSUMMARY COMPARISO